analysisWe need to parse the comment and produce an assert statement.

The method set(int index, E element) replaces element at index with element, returns old value. The comment: 

"@@@ If the index is valid (non-negative and less than the initial size of the list), then for every position greater than the index and within the list size, the element at that position remains unchanged if it was null before, or it is equal to its old value if it was not null."

Interpretation: For all positions i such that i > index && i < old size (initial size before modification), the element after set is same as before: if the old element was null, then after set it remains null; else after set it equals old value. That's essentially: for all i where index < i && i < \old(this.size()), this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)). Since if old value null, equality holds (null equals null). So we can just assert equality.

But need to include premise that index is valid: 0 <= index && index < \old(this.size()).

Thus assert: (0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))? Need to handle null: equals may throw NPE. Use ==? In Java spec, we can use == for reference equality, but for objects we may want Objects.equals. Use \old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). However typical examples use .equals. In earlier examples they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) assuming non-null. But here they mention null case. So we need to capture both cases. Could use ( \old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) ). Might be acceptable.

Simplify: assert (0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))));

We must reference elementData? Use public observer get(i). That's fine.

Thus final answer in code block.

