Comment:

// @@@ If the index is valid (non-negative and less than the initial size of the list), then for every position greater than the index and within the list size, the element at that position remains unchanged if it was null before, or it is equal to its old value if it was not null.

We need to refer to initial size (old size) before set. Also index validity: index >=0 && index < \old(this.size()).

We need to assert for all positions i such that i > index && i < this.size()? Wait after set size unchanged, so size same as old. So condition: i > index && i < this.size() (or <=? "greater than the index and within the list size" means i > index && i < this.size()? Since indices are 0..size-1 inclusive. So i > index && i < this.size() (i <= size-1). Equivalent i > index && i < this.size().

Now statement about element at that position remains unchanged if it was null before, or it is equal to its old value if it was not null.

We need to express: For each such i, either \old(this.get(i)) == null && this.get(i) == null (unchanged) OR \old(this.get(i)) != null && this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))).

But "remains unchanged if it was null before, or it is equal to its old value if it was not null." Actually unchanged means same value, which for null means still null. So we can express: this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . However equals may be null -> need null-safe. Use (this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i))) . That's fine for reference equality; but for objects we want equality semantics maybe Objects.equals. However we only have observer methods: get(i) returns E, we can use ==? In specification we can use (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

Simplify using Objects.equals? Not an observer method. Use equality check with .equals, but need null guard.

Thus assert:

(index >=0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; i > index && i < this.size(); (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))))

But need to ensure i within list size after operation same as old size. Use this.size() (post) which equals \old(this.size()).

Thus final assert.

